\pard\f2\b\fs28\fi-1020\li1200\fc0\cf0 Class Description\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 The NSCell class provides a mechanism for displaying text or images in an NSView without the overhead of a full NSView subclass. In particular, it provides much of the functionality of the NSText class by providing access to a shared NSText object used by all instances of NSCell in an application. NSCells are also extremely useful for placing text or images at various locations in a custom subclass of NSView.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 NSCell is used heavily by most of the NSControl classes to implement their internal workings. For example, NSSlider uses an NSSliderCell, NSTextField uses an NSTextFieldCell, and NSBrowser uses an NSBrowserCell. Sending a message to the NSControl is often simpler than dealing directly with the corresponding NSCell. For instance, NSControls typically invoke 
\b updateCell:
\b0  (causing the cell to be displayed) after changing a cell attribute; whereas if you directly call the corresponding method of the NSCell, the NSCell might not automatically display itself again.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 Some subclasses of NSControl (notably NSMatrix) group NSCells in an arrangement where they act together in some cooperative manner. Thus, with an NSMatrix, you can implement a uniformly sized group of radio buttons without needing an NSView for each button (and without needing an NSText object as the field editor for the text on each button).\

\fs14 \

\fs28 The NSCell class provides primitives for displaying text or an image, editing text, setting and getting object values, maintaining state, highlighting, and tracking the mouse. NSCell's method 
\b trackMouse:inRect:ofView:untilMouseUp:
\b0  implements the mechanism that sends action messages to target objects. However, NSCell implements target/action features abstractly, deferring the details of implementation to NSActionCell and its subclasses.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Object Values and Formatters\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Every NSCell that displays text has a value associated with it. The NSCell stores that value as an object of potentially any type, displays it as an NSString, and returns it as a primary value or string object, according to what's requested (
\b intValue
\b0 , 
\b floatValue
\b0 , 
\b stringValue
\b0 , and so on). Formatters are objects associated with NSCells (through 
\b setFormatter:
\b0 ) that translate a cell's object value to its it textual representation and that convert what users type into the underlying object. NSCells have built-in formatters to handle common string and numeric (
\b int
\b0 , 
\b float
\b0 , 
\b double
\b0 ) translations. In addition, you can specify date and numeric types more precisely with  
\b setEntryType:
\b0  and specify floating-point format characteristics with 
\b setFloatingPointFormat:left:right:
\b0 . You can also implement your own formatters to provide specialized object translation; see the NSFormatter specification for more information. \

\fs14 \

\fs28 The text that an NSCell displays and stores can be an attributed string. Several methods help to set and get attributed-string values, including s
\b etAttributedStringValue:
\b0  and 
\b setImportsGraphics:
\b0 . \

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs24\li1500\fc0\cf0 Represented Objects\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Represented objects are objects that an NSCell "stands for." (They're not to be confused with an NSCell's object value, which 
\i is
\i0  the value of the cell.) By setting a represented object for an NSCell (using 
\b setRepresentedObject:
\b0 ) you make an association between the NSCell and that object. For instance, you could have a pop-up list, each cell of which lists a color as its title; when the user selects a cell, the represented NSColor object is displayed in a color well.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Subclassing NSCell\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 The 
\b initImageCell:
\b0  method is the designated initializer for NSCells that display images. The 
\b initTextCell:
\b0  method is the designated initializer for NSCells that display text. Override one or both of these methods if you implement a subclass of NSCell that performs its own initialization. If you need to use target and action behavior, you may prefer to subclass NSActionCell or one of its subclasses, which provide the default implementation of this behavior.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 If you want to implement your own mouse-tracking or mouse-up behavior, consider overriding 
\b startTrackingAt:inView:
\b0 , 
\b continueTracking:at:inView:
\b0 , and 
\b stopTracking:at:inView:mouseIsUp:
\b0 . If you want to implement your own drawing, override 
\b drawWithFrame:inView:
\b0  or 
\b drawInteriorWithFrame:inView:
\b0 .\

\fs14 \

\fs28 For more information on how NSCell is used, see the NSControl class specification. \

\fs14 \

