\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Sets whether floating-point numbers are autoranged in the receiver, and sets the sizes of the fields to the left and right of the decimal point. If 
\i autoRange
\i0  is NO, 
\i leftDigits
\i0  specifies the maximum number of digits to the left of the decimal point, and 
\i rightDigits
\i0  specifies the number of digits to the right (the fractional digit places will be padded with zeros to fill this width).  However, if a number is too large to fit its integer part in 
\i leftDigits
\i0  digits, as many places as are needed on the left are effectively removed from 
\i rightDigits
\i0  when the number is displayed.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 If 
\i autoRange
\i0  is YES, 
\i leftDigits
\i0  and 
\i rightDigits
\i0  are simply added to form a maximum total field width for the receiver (plus 1 for the decimal point).  The fractional part will be padded with zeros on the right to fill this width, or truncated as much as possible (up to removing the decimal point and displaying the number as an integer).  The integer portion of a number is never truncated–that is, it is displayed in full no matter what the field width limit is.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 The following example sets a cell used to display dollar amounts up to 99,999.99:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0     [[currencyDollarsField cell] setEntryType:NSFloatType];\
    [[currencyDollarsField cell] setFloatingPointFormat:NO left:5 right:2];  \

\fs14 \

